\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Do absolute constants $\epsilon >0$ and $C>0$ exist such that every n-point set in $R^2$ has at most C $n^{4/3-\epsilon }$ unordered pairs at Euclidean distance one?

\subsection{Short English statement}
Do absolute constants $\epsilon >0$ and $C>0$ exist such that every n-point set in $R^2$ has at most C $n^{4/3-\epsilon }$ unordered pairs at Euclidean distance one?

